1. write out the optimized Singleton Version and explain each line of code
public final class Singleton {
    
    private Singleton() {
        if (Holder.INSTANCE != null) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("Instance already exists!");
        }
    }

    public static Singleton getInstance() {
        return Holder.INSTANCE;
    }

    private static class Holder {
        private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
    }
}
Line-by-Line Explanation
public final class Singleton {
Declares a public class named Singleton. Making it final prevents subclassing, which could otherwise compromise the singleton property.
private Singleton() {
The constructor is marked private so that no external code can instantiate this class using the new keyword.
if (Holder.INSTANCE != null) { throw new IllegalStateException("..."); }
An advanced safety check. It prevents malicious or accidental creation of a second instance via Java Reflection. If someone tries to force access to the private constructor, this throws an error.
public static Singleton getInstance() {
The global access point for the single instance. It is static, meaning you call it directly via Singleton.getInstance().
return Holder.INSTANCE;
Returns the single instance managed by the inner static class.
private static class Holder {
A private nested static class. The JVM will not load this class into memory when Singleton is first loaded. It only loads when getInstance() is called and references Holder, achieving lazy initialization.
private static final Singleton INSTANCE = new Singleton();
Instantiates the unique Singleton object. Because class loading is guaranteed by the JVM to be thread-safe, this instantiation is 100% thread-safe without needing expensive synchronized keywords.


2. what is reflection
In plain terms, Reflection is Java’s ability to "look in the mirror" and inspect or modify its own behavior at runtime.
Normally, code behavior is set in stone at compile time. With Reflection, your code can dynamically analyze a class, find out what fields, methods, and constructors it has, and even force access to things that 
are marked private. It is incredibly powerful but acts as a double-edged sword: it is heavily used by frameworks like Spring and Hibernate to magically wire components together, but it bypasses type-safety and
carries a minor performance cost.


3. what are http status code, 200/ 201/202/ 204/ 307/ 301/ 400/ 401/ 403/ 404/ 500, explain them by your own words
2xx Success (Everything went smoothly)
200 OK: The standard "Success!" code. The server got the request and sent back the data you asked for.
201 Created: Success, and something new was built. Usually happens after a POST request (e.g., you successfully registered a new account).
202 Accepted: The server heard you and agreed to do the job, but it hasn't finished yet. It's used for long, asynchronous tasks background processes.
204 No Content: Success, but there is nothing to send back in the body. You updated or deleted something, and the job is done.
3xx Redirection (You need to look elsewhere)
301 Moved Permanently: The URL you requested has changed for good. Your browser will automatically go to the new address from now on.
307 Temporary Redirect: The URL is currently somewhere else right now, but it might come back later. Crucially, the browser must use the exact same HTTP method (like POST) to hit the new location.
4xx Client Errors (You made a mistake)
400 Bad Request: The server can't understand what you want because the syntax or data you sent is messed up.
401 Unauthorized: You forgot your ID. You aren't logged in, or your security token is missing/expired.
403 Forbidden: The server knows who you are, but you don't have permission to look at this. (e.g., a standard user trying to access an Admin panel).
404 Not Found: The classic error. The server cannot find the specific webpage or resource you requested.
5xx Server Errors (The server messed up)
500 Internal Server Error: A generic catch-all. The server ran into an unexpected problem or a crash in its code and couldn't finish your request.


4. what is http  
HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the universal language of the World Wide Web. It is a stateless, application-layer protocol that determines exactly how messages (requests and responses) are formatted
and transmitted across the internet. Whenever you type a URL into a browser, your browser uses HTTP to request data from a web server, which then ships the data back to you using the same protocol.


5. what is get, post, put, delete, patch method
GET: Used to read or retrieve data. It should never alter data on the server.
POST: Used to create new data. The data is wrapped up inside the body of the request.
PUT: Used to replace an existing resource entirely. If the resource doesn't exist, it usually creates it.
DELETE: Used to remove a specific resource from the server.
PATCH: Used to make a partial update to a resource. You only send the specific fields you want to change.


6. post vs patch
POST creates a completely brand-new entity every single time you call it.
PATCH modifies existing data by applying a small fix or update to a specific part of it (like changing just a user's phone number).


7. post vs put
POST is used when you don't know the exact ID of the resource yet; the server creates it and assigns the ID. Calling it 5 times creates 5 separate records.
PUT is used when you know the exact location or ID of the resource. You supply the entire updated record. If you run it 5 times, the first one updates it and the next 4 just overwrite it with identical data.


8. What is idempotent, which http method is idempotent?
An HTTP method is idempotent if making identical requests multiple times produces the exact same side-effects on the server as making it a single time. In short: repeating the request won't change the state of
the database any further.
Idempotent or not:
GET: Yes. Reading data multiple times doesn't change anything.
PUT: Yes. Overwriting data with the exact same record multiple times results in the exact same database state.
DELETE: Yes. Deleting something once deletes it. Deleting it again keeps it deleted (even if the response code changes from 200 to 404, the server state remains unchanged).
OPTIONS / HEAD: Yes. These only read metadata.
POST: No. If you hit "Submit Order" five times, you might get charged five times and create five orders.
PATCH: Generally No. While it can be designed to be idempotent, it isn't guaranteed by definition. For example, if a PATCH request says "age": age + 1, repeating it will continuously alter the server state.

