1. TCP 3 way handshaking
The 3-way handshake is the process TCP uses to establish a reliable connection between a client and a server before they actually start swapping data. It ensures both sides are ready to talk and listen.
Step 1: SYN (Synchronize) — The client sends a packet with a SYN flag set to the server. It essentially says: "Hey, I want to talk to you. Here is my starting sequence number."
Step 2: SYN-ACK (Synchronize-Acknowledge) — The server receives it and replies with a packet containing both SYN and ACK flags. It says: "I hear you! I acknowledge your request. I also want to establish a connection
with you—here is my starting sequence number."
Step 3: ACK (Acknowledge) — The client receives the reply and sends a final ACK packet back. It says: "Got it! Let's start talking." The connection is now established.


2. TCP vs UDP
TCP (Transmission Control Protocol) — The Reliable, Connection-Oriented Protocol
Connection Mechanism: TCP is strictly connection-oriented. Before any data can be transferred, it forces the client and server to establish a dedicated communication channel via the "3-way handshake."
Reliability: It is 100% reliable. TCP acts like a premium courier service; it tracks every single packet by assigning a sequence number. If a packet gets lost in transit, TCP automatically requests a retransmission. 
If packets arrive out of order, it reassembles them correctly. It also manages flow and congestion control so the sender doesn't overwhelm the receiver.
Speed and Overhead: It is slower. The constant tracking, acknowledgments (ACKs), error-checking, and large packet header size create a noticeable amount of network overhead.
Use Cases: Web browsing (HTTP/HTTPS), Email (SMTP), and File Transfers (FTP). In these scenarios, losing even a single byte of data can corrupt an entire file or ruin a webpage.

UDP (User Datagram Protocol) — The Fast, Connectionless Protocol
Connection Mechanism: UDP is entirely connectionless. It does not perform a handshake. It simply packages the data and fires it over the network immediately, regardless of whether the receiver is active or ready.
Reliability: It is unreliable. UDP operates on a "best-effort" delivery model. It does not track packets, does not notify the sender if a packet is lost, and does not correct the order of arrival. If a packet drops, it is gone forever.
Speed and Overhead: It is extremely fast. Because it cuts out all flow control, error checking, and acknowledgment loops, it has minimal latency and a very lightweight packet header.
Use Cases: Live video streaming, online multiplayer gaming, video conferencing (VoIP), and DNS lookups. In these environments, real-time speed is far more critical than perfect data accuracy. Missing a single frame of a live video is 
unnoticeable, but pausing the entire video stream to wait for a retransmitted packet would ruin the user experience.


3. what is Tomcat
Tomcat is an open-source web server and Java Servlet Container. Standard web servers only understand static files (HTML, CSS, images). Tomcat acts as an engine that can actually execute Java backend code (Servlets and JSPs),
convert that execution into web-friendly format, and serve it out dynamically to users over HTTP.


4. what are the basic components for tomcat
Server: Represents the entire Tomcat JVM instance. It controls the lifecycle of the whole runtime container.
Service: A structural wrapper inside the Server that binds one or more Connectors to a single Engine.
Connector: The component that handles the incoming network traffic. It listens on a specific port (like 8080), receives requests from browsers, and hands them off to the engine.
Engine: The core request-processing heart of Tomcat. It takes the requests from the Connector and figures out which web application should handle them.
Host: Represents a virtual host (like localhost or example.com), allowing a single Tomcat instance to manage multiple domains.
Context: Represents an individual web application running inside a Host.


5. what is web server
A Web Server is computer software (and underlying hardware) that stores web site assets and delivers them to end-users across the internet via HTTP. At its simplest, when a browser requests a file, a web server finds that 
static file on its hard drive and serves it back. Modern web servers also act as reverse proxies, routing traffic to application servers.


6. what is 3 tire architecture
3-Tier Architecture is a software design pattern that breaks an application into three distinct, decoupled layers to make code easier to maintain, scale, and secure.
Presentation Tier (Frontend): The user interface. This is what the user interacts with (e.g., HTML/CSS/JavaScript webpages, mobile UI). It talks to the next layer via API calls.
Application/Logic Tier (Backend): The brains of the operation. This layer processes business rules, runs calculations, evaluates conditions, and decides what data needs to move where (e.g., Java/Spring Boot application running on Tomcat).
Data Tier (Database): The storage system. It stores, retrieves, and updates application data securely (e.g., MySQL, PostgreSQL).


7. what is OSI Model, what is each layer doing
The OSI (Open Systems Interconnection) Model is a conceptual framework that standardizes how data moves across a network, splitting the process into 7 distinct layers.
Layer 7: Application — Where the user interacts with network applications (e.g., HTTP, FTP, SMTP protocols).
Layer 6: Presentation — Translates, encrypts, and compresses data so it's readable by the application layer (e.g., SSL/TLS, JSON/XML parsing).
Layer 5: Session — Manages, maintains, and terminates communication sessions between two endpoints.
Layer 4: Transport — Coordinates reliable data transfer, flow control, and error correction (e.g., TCP and UDP live here).
Layer 3: Network — Finds the path to move data packets from one device to another across different networks (e.g., IP routing and routers live here).
Layer 2: Data Link — Manages node-to-node data transfer on the same local network, handling physical addressing (e.g., MAC addresses and network switches).
Layer 1: Physical — The literal hardware level. Deals with transmitting raw bits over cables, fiber optics, or radio waves.
